  "Number": 21,
  "Question": "If \\[\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty} \\cos^{2n} \\theta = 5,\\] what is the value of \\(\\cos 2\\theta?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{5}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Let \\(r=\\cos^2\\theta\\). The given series is\n\\[\n1+r+r^2+\\cdots=5.\n\\]\nHere \\(r\\ge0\\), and its finite sum forces \\(r<1\\): if \\(r\\ge1\\), every term would be at least \\(1\\). Thus the infinite geometric-series formula applies, with a nonzero denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{1-r}=5,\n\\qquad 1-r=\\frac15,\n\\qquad r=\\frac45.\n\\]\nThe double-angle identity uses this square directly, so we do not need to determine the sign of \\(\\cos\\theta\\):\n\\[\n\\cos 2\\theta=2\\cos^2\\theta-1\n=2\\cdot\\frac45-1\n=\\boxed{\\frac35}.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\text{D}\\).",
